essage before it where every message between them, at least one, holds only tool_result blocks for that earlier message's calls. The two are then sent to the model as one message, its calls followed by their results, so a client does this only for calls of one Messages API response that it ran one after another. A request that names calls to run is refused (invalid_handoff) unless its first message is a user message, not empty and not a tool_result."),tool_use_ids:C(o()).max(16).describe("@internal The tool_use blocks of the last message to run, each of a tool named in the turn_handoff entry of the worker external_metadata; all run in one batch, then the model continues once. May be empty, and then nothing runs and no model turn follows: with every call the turn carries answered within it (the last message holding none), the worker appends the turn as history and, once the lines are stored, echoes the last assistant message that holds a call; with stopped, the user stopped the turn before its calls ran. In both cases, when a leading part of the turn is already in the conversation from an earlier delivery, only the rest is appended; where its first assistant message repeats the id of the last one already there, as messages allows, the two are sent to the model as one message."),stopped:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Only true, and only with an empty tool_use_ids: the user stopped this turn while its last message, an assistant message holding calls that have not run, was being handed over. The worker appends the turn, closes those calls and marks the turn exactly as when a turn of its own is stopped before a call runs, and ends it with the result a stopped turn ends with; when an earlier delivery already appended the turn, the calls are closed in place."),relay_marker:u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("upgrade_relay_marker"),uuid:o(),content:o()}).optional().describe("@internal The system line that starts the prompt-cache relay for this conversation: uuid is the line's uuid, and content is the marker, a JSON string whose anchor_line_uuid is the uuid of the first of messages; other fields of the line are ignored. Only a worker that advertises relay_marker: true in turn_handoff_available uses it, on a request that names calls to run, or on a history request (no calls named, not stopped) if the session holds none of its messages yet. It is never a reason to refuse the turn: when the member is not such a line, or the worker does not use the marker, the request is answered as without it."),memory_context:u({uuid:o(),version:o().nullable(),content:o().nullable()}).optional().describe("@internal The snapshot of the user's claude.ai memory that the client showed its model with the turn's user message. Used on a request that names calls to run, or on a history request (no calls named, not stopped) if the session holds none of its messages yet: the worker writes it ahead of the first of messages, with uuid as the line's uuid, as a cowork_memory_context attachment line like those it writes for snapshots it fetches itself, and then adds no copy of that version. version and content are both strings for a snapshot, both null to withdraw an earlier one. The member is never a reason to refuse the request: one that is malformed, that has a version without content or the reverse, or whose uuid is also that of one of messages, of relay_marker or of a line the session's transcript holds, is ignored, and the turn goes on without the line."),message_client_platform:o().optional().describe("@internal The `anthropic-client-platform` value of the app a person sent the first of messages from. Left out when no person sent that message, as in a scheduled run. When it names one of the Remote Control apps (isRemoteControlAppPlatform) and that message is not one from another session, the worker marks the message as sent by a person, as it marks a user message that arrives with that value, so the turn counts as started by a person for the calls it runs and for those the model makes afterwards, and the turn the worker runs carries that value as its client platform, as a turn for such a user message does (per-turn telemetry, and tools that read which app the person is on). The member is never a reason to refuse the request: without it, or with any other value, the message is appended unmarked and the turn carries no platform."),file_names:C(u({file_uuid:o(),name:o()})).max(16).optional().describe("@internal Files a person attached to the conversation, each with the name the client showed its model for it, the most wanted first; at most 16. file_uuid is that of a file_attachments entry on a user message sent to this worker before the request. Only a worker that advertises file_names: true in turn_handoff_available uses the member, only on a request that names calls to run, and only when those calls run in the session's home directory: before they run, it copies each listed file it has downloaded into that directory, under that name, until a limit on time or on total size is reached, taking first the names that occur in the calls' own input. It replaces nothing then, skips a name it refuses or that the list holds twice, and, within one request, skips the further names of a file once it has found or placed one of them. If a person later sends another file under a name it placed, it puts that file's bytes in the copy's place, or removes the copy if it cannot, and only while the copy is still the file it placed, unchanged. The member is never a reason to refuse the request: a malformed one (a longer list included) is ignored whole, and the calls run whatever became of the copies.")}).describe('@internal Sent by the session client to a worker that advertises turn_handoff in its external_metadata: hand over a turn whose final assistant message holds tool calls that have not run. The worker answers at once \u2014 success {status: "accepted"} (validated and queued; the messages are appended at the start of the turn that runs the calls, the last one echoed back as an assistant event before any call runs; a request that runs nothing (see tool_use_ids) for the same turn as one still queued to run replaces it), success {status: "duplicate"} (every named call is already taken by an earlier delivery or answered; nothing runs \u2014 taken, not necessarily ran; or, for a turn that runs nothing, its calls are already answered by other lines, or the same turn is already pending in that shape or running), or an error whose message starts with its code and a colon: turn_handoff_disabled, invalid_handoff, unsupported_tool, unknown_tool, recovered_by_restart (this worker restarted after the turn was appended and its recovery set the turn\'s unrun calls aside; this request will not run or close them), expired (the request names calls to run and the session service has held it for over 30 minutes, by the created_at of the frame that delivered it: the service hands a request that no worker answered to every later worker of the session; nothing is appended or taken, and a request that runs nothing is never refused for its age). Accepted calls get the permission evaluation of the worker (rules, the permission mode, can_use_tool to the client); their tool_result messages and the model continuation follow on the event stream as in any turn.')),$Y=f(()=>u({status:U(["accepted","duplicate"]),tool_use_ids:C(o()).optional().describe("@internal The calls queued to run (status accepted).")}).describe("@internal Success payload answering turn_handoff.")),ma=f(()=>u({subtype:T("mcp_message"),server_name:o(),message:iR()}).describe("Carries one MCP JSON-RPC message for an SDK-hosted MCP server (one named in initialize.sdkMcpServers or added later with mcp_set_servers). Flows in both directions: the CLI sends it to the client to reach the in-process server, and the client sends it to the CLI to deliver that server's own messages. When the client answers, the success response carries the server's JSON-RPC reply under mcp_