nvoke-RestMethod`, .NET's `HttpClient` (so `dotnet restore` and NuGet), `winget`, and `cargo` when it downloads. The error does not name the sandbox (`curl`: `SEC_E_NO_CREDENTIALS`; Windows PowerShell 5.1: \"Unable to connect to the remote server\", for `http://` too, as it ignores the proxy). Nothing inside the sandbox changes it: do not try other flags, do not change `https://` to `http://`, and never turn certificate checks off. Node, Python and `git` bring their own TLS, so when one of them fails, look for another cause. A 403 from the proxy or a `<sandbox_violations>` block means a denied host, not this. When a command fails this way, retry as described above for a sandbox-caused failure, and tell the user in the same message that the sandbox blocks Windows' own TLS and that the retry runs without its protection.":`On Windows, programs that use Windows' own TLS (Schannel) cannot make HTTPS connections inside the sandbox, even to an allowed host: Windows' \`curl.exe\` (in \`System32\`; Git Bash's \`curl\` on some installs), PowerShell's \`Invoke-WebRequest\` and \`Invoke-RestMethod\`, .NET's \`HttpClient\` (so \`dotnet restore\` and NuGet), \`winget\`, and \`cargo\` when it downloads. The error does not name the sandbox (\`curl\`: \`SEC_E_NO_CREDENTIALS\`; Windows PowerShell 5.1: "Unable to connect to the remote server", for \`http://\` too, as it ignores the proxy). Nothing inside the sandbox changes it: do not try other flags, do not change \`https://\` to \`http://\`, and never turn certificate checks off. Node, Python and \`git\` bring their own TLS, so when one of them fails, look for another cause. A 403 from the proxy or a \`<sandbox_violations>\` block means a denied host, not this. When a command fails this way, tell the user that the sandbox blocks Windows' own TLS, so the command works only outside the sandbox, without its protection: they can ${Kt}; leaving the sandbox is their decision, not yours.`);let no=[];if(O()==="windows"){let $n=`${bn}, which